# Provenance — charts/README.md § `SOURCE` is required, including when nothing was forked.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS THE FIRST CHART IN charts/managed/ WITH NO UPSTREAM AT ALL, AND THE FILE IS HERE ANYWAY
# BECAUSE ITS ABSENCE WOULD BE INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM AN OVERSIGHT. Build.Charts' own remarks make
# that argument: "there is no SOURCE file" is a legal state only if it is written down. Every other
# chart here wraps somebody else's operator and records which commit it read; this one renders four
# built-in Kubernetes objects and an image the platform builds itself, so the provenance that matters
# is the API contract, not a third party's release.

vendored: none
upstream: none

# ⚠ WHAT WAS ACTUALLY READ, AND WHAT IS ASSUMED. Every claim below is about the Kubernetes API, which
# is the only upstream this row has.
upstream-api: v1 (PersistentVolumeClaim, ServiceAccount, Pod), networking.k8s.io/v1 (NetworkPolicy)

reviewed: 2026-08-18

# ── What the review established ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ A NetworkPolicy HAS NO `deny` RULE AND docs/plan/19 ASKS FOR ONE. That document's § The pod wants
# "a NetworkPolicy denying access to the platform's own namespaces". `NetworkPolicyEgressRule` is an
# allow-list: a policy selects pods and lists what they MAY reach, and a pod selected by any policy is
# denied everything not listed. So "everything except namespace X" has no spelling — a rule allowing
# 0.0.0.0/0 allows the platform's pods too, because their addresses are in the cluster CIDR. The
# requirement is met by allowing the tenant's own namespaces positively and cutting every private
# range out of the public rule, so the platform is excluded by construction. Recorded here because the
# natural reading of that sentence produces a policy that silently allows what it was written to
# forbid.
#
# ⚠ `ipBlock` MAY NOT BE COMBINED WITH A `namespaceSelector` IN ONE PEER. NetworkPolicyPeer's own API
# reference: ipBlock "cannot be used with namespaceSelector or podSelector". That is why the rendered
# policy has four separate egress rules rather than one rule with four peers, and it is why the count
# is what CloudConsoles.Matches compares.
#
# ⚠ `kubernetes.io/metadata.name` IS SET BY THE API SERVER ON EVERY NAMESPACE. The NamespaceDefaultLabelName
# feature went GA in Kubernetes 1.22 and the label is applied by the apiserver's namespace strategy,
# not by whoever created the namespace. That is the only reason the "reach my own namespace" rule works
# today: `cybercloud.io/tenant-id` on a namespace is applied by NOTHING in this repository — see
# conformance.yaml § owed, `namespaces-are-not-labelled`.
#
# ⚠ `persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy` IS A PersistentVolume FIELD, NOT A PersistentVolumeClaim ONE, and
# it is defaulted from the StorageClass. A claim therefore CANNOT ask for its bytes to outlive it. That
# is why this type's delete is irreversible whatever the provider does, and it is the counter-argument
# to declining soft delete — conformance.yaml § owed, `delete-takes-the-home-directory`.
#
# ⚠ THE DEFAULT StorageClass IN k3s BINDS WITH `volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer`. local-path
# is k3s' bundled provisioner and its StorageClass carries that mode, so a claim with no pod scheduled
# against it stays Pending indefinitely — correctly. A console deliberately has no pod until somebody
# attaches, so a readiness gate on `status.phase == "Bound"` would deadlock: converge waits for the
# pod, and the pod is refused until converge. This is the single fact that decides what Converged means
# on this row.
#
# ⚠ `allowPrivilegeEscalation` DEFAULTS TO TRUE. SecurityContext's own API reference: "Defaults to
# true", except when runAsNonRoot is true AND the container is privileged, which is not this pod's
# shape. It is written explicitly for that reason and not for symmetry.
#
# ⚠ A POD WITH NO `serviceAccountName` RUNS AS `default` WITH ITS TOKEN MOUNTED. That is the unsafe
# default this row had to close, and a shell containing kubectl is the worst possible place to leave it
# open. `automountServiceAccountToken: false` is set on BOTH the ServiceAccount and the Pod, because
# the pod-level field is the one that binds and the account-level one is the one an auditor reads.
#
# ⚠ `activeDeadlineSeconds` IS ENFORCED BY THE KUBELET AND IS THE ONLY HALF OF docs/plan/19's SESSION
# POLICY THAT ANYTHING ENFORCES TODAY. The idle timeout is a keystroke question and is carried as an
# annotation for a reaper that does not exist yet.
#
# ── The weak claims, named ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ THE PRIVATE-RANGE EXCEPTIONS ASSUME THE CLUSTER'S OWN CIDRs ARE PRIVATE. True for every cluster
# CyberCloud.ContainerService/managedClusters creates (10.244.0.0/16 and 10.96.0.0/12) and NOT
# checked for a BYO cluster. conformance.yaml § owed, `cluster-cidrs-are-assumed-private`.
#
# ⚠ NOBODY HAS CONFIRMED THAT THE SHELL IMAGE'S TOOLS WORK UNDER `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true` WITH
# ONLY $HOME AND /tmp WRITABLE. There is no image to try it against. Several of docs/plan/19's tools
# write outside both by default — helm to ~/.cache (covered), npm to a prefix that may not be, and
# anything using /var/tmp is not. conformance.yaml § owed, `no-image-pipeline`.
upstream-webhook: none — no admission webhook is installed for any of these kinds; what refuses this pod in a hardened cluster is Pod Security admission, which the rendered securityContext is written to satisfy at the `restricted` level
